{"id": "swebq0058", "pool": "swebq", "arm": "always", "tier": "always", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 1244407635, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "aa7a8a6f2040793a4fe8dabcb6d10f2520887b0e0988d37809c3a27ef31575d8", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 6, "query": "where did starbucks get their logo?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/swebq0058.wav", "input_sha256": "97984d2503e6a23dfeb1602d9f186b96a771ac4d7648068523b6f98056da8777", "input_samples": 34162, "input_s": 2.135, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.8717, "act": 0.998, "is_info": true, "fired": true, "mode": "escalated", "answer": "", "relay_text": "Starbucks got its logo from a twin-tailed siren, a mythological sea creature. The founders wanted a nautical theme because Starbucks was born in Seattle, a port city, and the company name came from Starbuck, the first mate in Moby-Dick. While looking through old marine books, they found an image of a two-tailed mermaid/siren and adapted it into the logo. So, the Starbucks logo comes from maritime mythology and old seafaring imagery, specifically a twin-tailed siren.", "expert_answer": "Starbucks got its logo from a **twin-tailed siren**, a mythological sea creature.\n\nThe founders wanted a nautical theme because **Starbucks was born in Seattle**, a port city, and the company name came from **Starbuck**, the first mate in *Moby-Dick*. While looking through old marine books, they found an image of a **two-tailed mermaid/siren** and adapted it into the logo.\n\nSo, the Starbucks logo comes from **maritime mythology and old seafaring imagery**, specifically a **twin-tailed siren**.", "transcript": "Where did Starbucks get their logo?\n", "asr_s": 0.51, "expert_s": 4.31, "expert_input_samples": 34162, "expert_input_s": 2.135, "expert_input_sha256": "97984d2503e6a23dfeb1602d9f186b96a771ac4d7648068523b6f98056da8777", "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": 3.831, "relay_synth_ms": 12233, "onset_chunk": 3, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 1, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"relay": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0058_always_relay.wav", "sha256": "2a4e20b855c03e469299d1b6d44bee5dd7270a908665fa959e0321d178a7b0b5", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 27.8}, "candidate": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0058_always_candidate.wav", "sha256": "e098723512ff70880518c46fa50088d16e5ceea638e8d5a397c65cf6810ad213", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 1.0}, "stall": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0058_always_stall.wav", "sha256": "55221c802d53ea199d3d574bc8d612b4b10486cc895be950d12dbba0650be5e9", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 2.2}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.055, 1.101, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.058, 2.102, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 2.135, 2.19, 2.232, 1, 0, 0], [3, 0, 3.135, 3.19, 3.648, 0, 24000, 0], [4, 0, 4.135, 4.2, 4.643, 0, 28800, 1]], "status": "completed", "error": null, "ts": {"feed_start": 0.0, "input_end": 2.135, "last_real_feed_done": 2.19, "onset": 3.648, "gate_read_done": 3.648, "fire": 3.648, "candidate_first_pcm": 3.648, "stall_note": 3.649, "stall_first_pcm": 4.1, "local_first_pcm": null, "wait_start": 4.643, "expert_req": 3.649, "expert_resp": 8.474, "relay_synth_start": 8.475, "relay_first_pcm": 9.118, "relay_synth_end": 20.708, "first_any_pcm": 3.648, "first_answer_pcm": 9.118, "session_end": 21.203}, "ttfa_answer_s": 6.983, "ttfa_any_s": 1.513}
{"id": "swebq0069", "pool": "swebq", "arm": "always", "tier": "always", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 369509382, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "aa7a8a6f2040793a4fe8dabcb6d10f2520887b0e0988d37809c3a27ef31575d8", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 6, "query": "what led to stalin rise in power?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/swebq0069.wav", "input_sha256": "9c9d1bd1d6699469011b26fa32cfe99e82f98ea206b2a6b14cd906ac2ed335c2", "input_samples": 37134, "input_s": 2.321, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.5842, "act": 0.9935, "is_info": true, "fired": true, "mode": "escalated", "answer": "", "relay_text": "Stalin’s rise to power was mainly due to a combination of party position, political maneuvering, and the weakness/removal of rivals. Key factors: Lenin’s death in 1924 Lenin’s death created a power struggle inside the Communist Party. There was no clear successor. , Stalin’s role as General Secretary Stalin became General Secretary of the Communist Party in 1922. In short, Stalin rose to power because he used his position in the Communist Party to build loyalty, outmaneuver rivals, and present himself as Lenin’s rightful successor after Lenin died.", "expert_answer": "Stalin’s rise to power was mainly due to a combination of **party position, political maneuvering, and the weakness/removal of rivals**.\n\nKey factors:\n\n1. **Lenin’s death in 1924**  \n   Lenin’s death created a power struggle inside the Communist Party. There was no clear successor.\n\n2. **Stalin’s role as General Secretary**  \n   Stalin became **General Secretary of the Communist Party in 1922**. This gave him control over party appointments, allowing him to place loyal supporters in important positions.\n\n3. **Political alliances and manipulation**  \n   Stalin formed temporary alliances with other Bolshevik leaders to isolate his rivals. He first worked with Zinoviev and Kamenev against Trotsky, then later turned against them too.\n\n4. **Defeat of Trotsky**  \n   Leon Trotsky was Stalin’s strongest rival, but Stalin portrayed him as disloyal and too radical. Trotsky was removed from power, expelled from the party, and eventually exiled.\n\n5. **“Socialism in One Country”**  \n   Stalin’s policy of building socialism inside the Soviet Union appealed to many party members more than Trotsky’s idea of permanent world revolution.\n\n6. **Control of the party machine**  \n   By controlling promotions, memberships, and bureaucracy, Stalin built a strong base of supporters who helped him outvote and remove opponents.\n\nIn short, **Stalin rose to power because he used his position in the Communist Party to build loyalty, outmaneuver rivals, and present himself as Lenin’s rightful successor after Lenin died**.", "transcript": "What led to Stalin rise in power?\n", "asr_s": 0.66, "expert_s": 6.62, "expert_input_samples": 37134, "expert_input_s": 2.321, "expert_input_sha256": "9c9d1bd1d6699469011b26fa32cfe99e82f98ea206b2a6b14cd906ac2ed335c2", "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": 6.268, "relay_synth_ms": 16930, "onset_chunk": 3, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 1, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"relay": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0069_always_relay.wav", "sha256": "3c518446b4fe08736cc855fae943859630d54688586b59ea908e62adc0f453ef", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 39.96}, "candidate": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0069_always_candidate.wav", "sha256": "caa95e28544ce7e3f91c140f6d495b90583ecb42b2b6c2feeb752643c68fa1d1", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 1.0}, "stall": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/swebq/audio/swebq0069_always_stall.wav", "sha256": "b397097bef6d905e940fcfa5e76ed00f337340c68a9557bdc207347e93bd95ea", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 2.16}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.055, 1.1, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.058, 2.104, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 2.321, 2.379, 2.424, 1, 0, 0], [3, 0, 3.321, 3.378, 3.822, 0, 24000, 0], [4, 0, 4.321, 4.378, 4.838, 0, 27840, 1]], "status": "completed", "error": null, "ts": {"feed_start": 0.0, "input_end": 2.321, "last_real_feed_done": 2.379, "onset": 3.822, "gate_read_done": 3.823, "fire": 3.823, "candidate_first_pcm": 3.822, "stall_note": 3.824, "stall_first_pcm": 4.293, "local_first_pcm": null, "wait_start": 4.838, "expert_req": 3.823, "expert_resp": 11.106, "relay_synth_start": 11.106, "relay_first_pcm": 11.727, "relay_synth_end": 28.037, "first_any_pcm": 3.822, "first_answer_pcm": 11.727, "session_end": 28.351}, "ttfa_answer_s": 9.406, "ttfa_any_s": 1.502}
{"id": "swebq0078", "pool": "swebq", "arm": "always", "tier": "always", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 2014987729, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "aa7a8a6f2040793a4fe8dabcb6d10f2520887b0e0988d37809c3a27ef31575d8", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 6, "query": "what colleges did albert einstein teach at?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/swebq0078.wav", "input_sha256": "43c1ce74569857f3048e9e2708a2227d86700ca11c94c65ab8c3bb0552d14211", "input_samples": 39363, "input_s": 2.46, "n_real_chunks": 3, "score": 0.5995, "act": 0.9988, "is_info": true, "fired": true, "mode": "escalated", "answer": "", "relay_text": "Albert Einstein taught or held professorships at several universities, including: University of Bern — lecturer/Privatdozent, 1908–1909 , University of Zurich — associate professor, 1909–1911 , German University in Prague — professor, 1911–1912 , ETH Zurich — professor, 1912–1914 , University of Berlin — professor, 1914–1933 Later, in the United States, he worked at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton, but that was not a college/university teaching post in the usual sense.", "expert_answer": "Albert Einstein taught or held professorships at several universities, including:\n\n- **University of Bern** — lecturer/Privatdozent, 1908–1909  \n- **University of Zurich** — associate professor, 1909–1911  \n- **German University in Prague** — professor, 1911–1912  \n- **ETH Zurich** — professor, 1912–1914  \n- **University of Berlin** — professor, 1914–1933  \n\nLater, in the United States, he worked at the **Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton**, but that was **not a college/university teaching post** in the usual sense.", "transcript": "What colleges did Albert Einstein teach at?\n", "asr_s": 0.59, "expert_s": 5.09, "expert_input_samples": 39363, "expert_input_s": 2.46, "expert_input_sha256": 